summary:
I swept spec/tarn.md (edition B, 5004 lines) against scratch/cleanroom/editions/a/tarn.md (edition A, 4810 lines), reading only those two files. Because both editions are hard-wrapped (blockquote lines carry a literal "> " per line), a raw diff is useless — a line-folded control diff showed ~6100 of ~5000 lines differing purely from re-wrapping. I instead normalized both files (strip blockquote markers, join each blank-line-delimited block into one line) and ran two complementary dynamic-programming sequence alignments that preserve document order rather than greedy nearest-match: one over every Note/Example/Rationale block (including extended-title ones like "Note — what slabs are, and are not.", which a first narrower regex pass missed), and one over every paragraph within each of the ~90 shared section headers (identical in both editions, confirmed by header grep). Both alignments were then manually read side by side for every low-scoring or unmatched pairing, at two similarity thresholds.

Results: the two editions are in very close agreement. Every one of the ten paragraphs B has with no direct A counterpart, on full-text reading, turned out to be either a paragraph-reflow (A keeps as one long paragraph what B splits for readability, e.g. §5.2, §9.2, §9.6, §9.7, §18.2, §20.5) or a cross-subsection relocation with identical substance (the R-SER-04 retirement note moves from B's §2.2 to A's §14.4; the metadata-compression/quarantine passage sits in B's §7.3 but A's §7.1; the verifier's "cannot establish" grounds list is stated once at B's §21.6 but once at A's §2.3). One paragraph — B's §15.4 Rationale on why tile-index segmentation exists — has no A counterpart at all, but every specific quantifier it cites (2^16-entry segments, the entry_count formula, the worked 1,000,000-entry example) is present identically in both editions' normative text, so no implementation would differ. Conversely A carries a handful of extra explanatory Notes B lacks (a preamble reading-guide Note, a §2.3 note illustrating the format-major "reach" rule with F5/F14/F15, a new §3.3 on Part I/II independence, a §4.4 cross-reference note, and extra closure language in A's §2.3 conformance-class definitions) — none of these represent A missing anything B decided, which is this sweep's actual question, so I record them as observations rather than findings against my charter. I found no case where A states a B-decided rule, carve-out, exception, quantifier, or worked figure more narrowly, more widely, only inside a non-normative block where B is normative, or not at all in any form; and no case where B decides something new that A's own text does not ground. Register tables, requirement-ID paragraphs, §3.1 term content, fenced diagrams, and plain tables were left to the mechanical checker per instructions, with only the terms' differing internal ordering and the §2/§3 section-order swap noted as a structural observation, not independently re-verified.</parameter>
<parameter name="evidence">{"sweeps_run": "Ran, in order: (1) a header-boundary check (grep on both files) confirming identical section headers and order; (2) a normalization pass stripping blockquote markers and joining each blank-line-delimited block's hard-wrapped lines into one, to neutralize wrap-only differences (confirmed necessary by a raw line-folded diff returning about 6100 of about 5000 differing lines); (3) a DP sequence alignment over every Note/Example/Rationale block, run twice - a first pass matching only bare-period labels found 82 B blocks vs 85 A blocks, a second pass widened to catch em-dash-subtitled blocks found all 90 B vs 94 A blocks and is the one relied on; (4) a DP sequence alignment at full-paragraph granularity within every shared section header, run at similarity thresholds 0.6 and then 0.8 to widen the net, flagging every below-threshold pair and every paragraph with no counterpart; (5) manual side-by-side raw-text reads of every pairing either run flagged, roughly 35 distinct comparisons across sections 1-9, 11-16, 18, 20, 21; (6) targeted grep sweeps across both full files for the specific quantifiers in B's unmatched section 15.4 Rationale (segment size, the entry-count formula, the MiB figures) to check whether they appear anywhere in A outside that Rationale - all of them do, in both editions' normative text and worked example, which is the control that closes that candidate as EQUIVALENT rather than LOSS.", "derived_numbers": "Block counts (90 labelled blocks in B, 94 in A) and normalized-paragraph counts (1163 lines for B, 1135 for A after normalization) are counts computed from my own normalization and alignment scripts; neither document states a total block or paragraph count to check these against, so they are working statistics internal to this sweep's methodology, not figures compared against a number either text states.", "rejected_candidates": "Several DP pairings scored very low and looked like real gaps before full-text reading: B's section 2.2 R-SER-04 note paired at only 0.33 with an unrelated A note (the true match, at 0.68, sits far away at A's section 14.4, closed by reading both in full); B's section 16.2 slab Rationale paired at 0.16, and was nearly reported as a true gap before noticing the block-regex had skipped A's em-dash-subtitled Note entirely - re-running with a wider pattern found the real, content-identical match. Both were opened on a suspiciously low score and closed by direct full-text reading rather than trusted at face value.", "confidence_empty": "Zero LOSS, ADDITION, or CONTRADICTION findings survived manual verification. Where the automated sweep found little, I did not stop at the first clean-looking score: I re-ran the block census with a widened label pattern after noticing a block my first pass should have caught but didn't; I re-ran the paragraph sweep at two thresholds (0.6, then 0.8) specifically to check a too-strict cutoff wasn't hiding real divergence; and for all ten B-only paragraphs across both runs I read the raw full-text context in both editions rather than accepting no-match as final, finding nine were reflow or relocation and grepping the tenth's specific quantifiers across both files to confirm its substance survives in A even though its explanatory paragraph does not."}
